 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Notebook 12 – K-Means Clustering\n",
    "\n",
    "## Topics Covered\n",
    "- K-Means\n",
    "- Centroids\n",
    "- Distance\n",
    "- Cluster Assignment\n",
    "- Centroid Update\n",
    "- Iterations\n",
    "- Choosing K\n",
    "- Elbow Method\n",
    "- Silhouette Score\n",
    "- Cluster Interpretation\n",
    "- Cluster Visualization"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. What is K-Means?\n",
    "\n",
    "K-Means is an unsupervised machine learning algorithm used for clustering.\n",
    "\n",
    "It divides data into K groups called clusters.\n",
    "\n",
    "K = number of clusters.\n",
    "\n",
    "The algorithm tries to keep similar data points together."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. How K-Means Works\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Choose the number of clusters K.\n",
    "2. Initialize K centroids.\n",
    "3. Calculate the distance between each point and each centroid.\n",
    "4. Assign each point to the nearest centroid.\n",
    "5. Calculate new centroids using the mean of points in each cluster.\n",
    "6. Repeat the process until the clusters become stable."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Distance\n",
    "\n",
    "K-Means commonly uses Euclidean distance.\n",
    "\n",
    "For two points P(x1, y1) and Q(x2, y2):\n",
    "\n",
    "Distance = sqrt((x1 - x2)^2 + (y1 - y2)^2)\n",
    "\n",
    "The point is assigned to the centroid with the smallest distance."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "\n",
    "from sklearn.datasets import make_blobs\n",
    "from sklearn.cluster import KMeans\n",
    "from sklearn.metrics import silhouette_score"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Create Sample Data\n",
    "\n",
    "We create an unlabeled dataset containing three natural groups."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "X, _ = make_blobs(\n",
    "    n_samples=300,\n",
    "    centers=3,\n",
    "    cluster_std=1.0,\n",
    "    random_state=42\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Dataset shape:\", X.shape)\n",
    "print(\"First 5 rows:\")\n",
    "print(X[:5])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Visualize Original Data"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "plt.figure(figsize=(8, 6))\n",
    "plt.scatter(X[:, 0], X[:, 1], s=40)\n",
    "plt.title(\"Original Unlabeled Data\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Feature 1\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Feature 2\")\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. Apply K-Means\n",
    "\n",
    "Here we use K = 3."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "kmeans = KMeans(\n",
    "    n_clusters=3,\n",
    "    random_state=42,\n",
    "    n_init=10\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "cluster_labels = kmeans.fit_predict(X)\n",
    "centroids = kmeans.cluster_centers_"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 7. Cluster Assignment and Centroids"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(\"First 20 cluster assignments:\")\n",
    "print(cluster_labels[:20])\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nCentroids:\")\n",
    "print(centroids)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 8. Visualize Resulting Clusters\n",
    "\n",
    "The X markers represent the cluster centroids."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "plt.figure(figsize=(8, 6))\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.scatter(\n    X[:, 0],\n    X[:, 1],\n    c=cluster_labels,\n    s=40\n    )\n\n",
    "plt.scatter(\n    centroids[:, 0],\n    centroids[:, 1],\n    marker=\"X\",\n    s=250,\n    label=\"Centroids\"\n    )\n\n",
    "plt.title(\"K-Means Clustering\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Feature 1\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Feature 2\")\n",
    "plt.legend()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 9. Iterations\n",
    "\n",
    "K-Means repeatedly assigns points and updates centroids until convergence."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(\"Number of iterations:\", kmeans.n_iter_)\n",
    "print(\"Maximum iterations allowed:\", kmeans.max_iter)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 10. Elbow Method\n",
    "\n",
    "The Elbow Method helps us choose an appropriate value of K.\n",
    "\n",
    "It calculates inertia for different values of K.\n",
    "\n",
    "Inertia is the sum of squared distances between data points and their assigned centroids.\n",
    "\n",
    "We look for the point where the decrease in inertia starts becoming smaller."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "inertias = []\n    \n",
    "for k in range(1, 11):\n",
    "    model = KMeans(\n",
    "        n_clusters=k,\n",
    "        random_state=42,\n",
    "        n_init=10\n",
    "    )\n",
    "    model.fit(X)\n",
    "    inertias.append(model.inertia_)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))\n",
    "plt.plot(range(1, 11), inertias, marker=\"o\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"Elbow Method\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Number of Clusters (K)\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Inertia\")\n",
    "plt.xticks(range(1, 11))\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 11. Silhouette Score\n",
    "\n",
    "The Silhouette Score measures how well data points fit within their assigned clusters.\n",
    "\n",
    "The score ranges from -1 to 1.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Close to 1 → well-separated clusters\n",
    "- Around 0 → overlapping clusters\n",
    "- Below 0 → possible incorrect assignments\n",
    "\n",
    "A higher score generally indicates better-defined clusters."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "score = silhouette_score(X, cluster_labels)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Silhouette Score:\", round(score, 4))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 12. Compare Different K Values\n",
    "\n",
    "We can calculate the Silhouette Score for different numbers of clusters."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "scores = []\n    \n",
    "for k in range(2, 11):\n",
    "    model = KMeans(\n    "        n_clusters=k,\n    "        random_state=42,\n    "        n_init=10\n    "    )\n    "    labels = model.fit_predict(X)\n    "    score = silhouette_score(X, labels)\n    "    scores.append(score)\n\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))\n",
    "plt.plot(range(2, 11), scores, marker=\"o\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"Silhouette Score for Different K Values\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Number of Clusters (K)\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Silhouette Score\")\n",
    "plt.xticks(range(2, 11))\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 13. Cluster Interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "K-Means produces cluster numbers such as 0, 1, and 2. These numbers do not have an inherent meaning.\n",
    "\n",
    "We need to examine the features of each cluster to understand what the groups represent.\n",
    "\n",
    "For example, in customer segmentation:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Cluster 0 could represent high-spending customers.\n",
    "- Cluster 1 could represent medium-spending customers.\n",
    "- Cluster 2 could represent low-spending customers.\n",
    "\n",
    "The actual interpretation depends on the dataset."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "unique_clusters, counts = np.unique(cluster_labels, return_counts=True)\n    \n",
    "print(\"Cluster sizes:\")\n",
    "\n",
    "for cluster, count in zip(unique_clusters, counts):\n",
    "    print(\"Cluster\", cluster, \":\", count, \"data points\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 14. Important Viva Points\n",
    "\n",
    "1. K-Means is an unsupervised learning algorithm.\n",
    "2. K represents the number of clusters.\n",
    "3. A centroid is the center of a cluster.\n",
    "4. Data points are assigned to the nearest centroid.\n",
    "5. Centroids are updated using the mean of cluster points.\n",
    "6. K-Means repeats assignment and centroid update until convergence.\n",
    "7. Euclidean distance is commonly used by K-Means.\n",
    "8. The Elbow Method uses inertia to help select K.\n",
    "9. Inertia decreases as K increases.\n",
    "10. Silhouette Score ranges from -1 to 1.\n",
    "11. A higher Silhouette Score generally indicates better-separated clusters.\n",
    "12. Cluster numbers do not have inherent meanings.\n",
    "13. Cluster interpretation requires examining the original features.\n",
    "14. Feature scaling can be important when features have different scales."
   ]
  }
 ],
